 ],
 "literal": "The chest of the chief of the agricultural tract you desire, I say to you / Do not grieve, may you live long, my friend! Nannan's / fragrant mango tree having cut down, from the country banished / like the Kosar of the united word, / firm determination and strategy is indeed needed a little.",
 "translation": "My friend, may you live long! Do not grieve because you desire the chest of your beloved lord. Just like the Kosar clan of unwavering words, who cut down the sacred mango tree of Nannan and banished him from his country, we too need a little bit of firm determination and clever strategy to achieve our goal.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! நீ வாழ்க! மருத நிலத் தலைவனின் மார்பினை நீ விரும்புகிறாய்; அதற்காக நீ வருந்தாதே. நன்னன் என்பவனுடைய நறுமணமிக்க மாமரத்தை வெட்டி வீழ்த்தி, அவனை அவனது நாட்டிலிருந்தே துரத்திய, தங்களது சொல்லில் மாறாத கோசர்களைப் போல, நமக்கும் சிறிதளவு உறுதியான மனமும் தந்திரமான சூழ்ச்சியும் தேவைப்படுகிறது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the companion to the heroine who is suffering from separation. The companion comforts her, telling her not to lose heart over her desire for the hero's chest. She draws a historical parallel to the Kosar clan, who were famous for keeping their word and acting with fierce determination. The Kosars punished the chieftain Nannan for his injustice by cutting down his tutelary mango tree and banishing him. The companion suggests that they too must employ a bit of firm resolve and clever planning to persuade the hero to marry her quickly. The key words are 'அழியல்' (do not grieve), 'ஒன்று மொழி' (unwavering word), and 'வன்கட் சூழ்ச்சி' (firm strategy).",
